From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] py/scheduler: A Ctrl-C that lands in a scheduled callback
 also stops the code it interrupted.

A Ctrl-C is raised at the next branch of whatever Python code runs, and
that is often a scheduled callback (a soft machine.Timer's, a
micropython.schedule() call's) rather than the command at the REPL. The
callback stops, mp_sched_run_pending() prints the KeyboardInterrupt and
carries on, and the command runs on. A program whose callbacks take most
of the time loses nearly every press: on an ESP32-S3 drawing animation
frames from a timer, a busy loop at the REPL needed 14 to 90 presses
over the UART. Over WebREPL the poll that reads the Ctrl-C (patch 0011)
runs from the scheduler as a C task, and a Python callback queued behind
it then runs in the same pass and takes the exception: one press stopped
the loop 4 times in 10. With this patch one press stopped it 10 times in
10 both ways.

Remember that mp_handle_pending() raised the Ctrl-C exception while a
Python callback ran, and once the callback returns, however it ended,
mark the Ctrl-C pending again, so the code the callback interrupted gets
it at its next branch or wait. A callback that caught it to clean up
(multimer keeps it for the program's next idle point) still stops the
command. A callback that never returns is still stopped by the Ctrl-C,
as before, and the command after it.

Only the main thread's code is sent a Ctrl-C, so a callback that runs on
another thread passes nothing on. A port whose signal handler raises the
Ctrl-C itself (MICROPY_ASYNC_KBD_INTR, the unix port) doesn't go through
mp_handle_pending() and is unchanged.

diff --git a/py/scheduler.c b/py/scheduler.c
index e2ebaaff1..cefce2503 100644
--- a/py/scheduler.c
+++ b/py/scheduler.c
@@ -44,6 +44,12 @@ void MICROPY_WRAP_MP_SCHED_EXCEPTION(mp_sched_exception)(mp_obj_t exc) {
     #endif
 }
 
+#if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION && MICROPY_ENABLE_SCHEDULER
+// Set when mp_handle_pending() raises the Ctrl-C exception, so the scheduler can
+// tell that one landed inside the Python callback it ran.
+static bool sched_kbd_raised;
+#endif
+
 #if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION
 // This function may be called asynchronously at any time so only do the bare minimum.
 void MICROPY_WRAP_MP_SCHED_KEYBOARD_INTERRUPT(mp_sched_keyboard_interrupt)(void) {
@@ -123,7 +129,24 @@ static inline void mp_sched_run_pending(void) {
         MP_STATE_VM(sched_idx) = IDX_MASK(MP_STATE_VM(sched_idx) + 1);
         --MP_STATE_VM(sched_len);
         MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
+        #if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION
+        sched_kbd_raised = false;
+        #endif
         mp_call_function_1_protected(item.func, item.arg);
+        #if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION
+        // A Ctrl-C raised inside the callback stopped only the callback, which
+        // may have printed it or kept it. It was meant for the code the callback
+        // interrupted, so raise it again there. Only the main thread's code gets
+        // a Ctrl-C, so a callback run on another thread passes nothing on.
+        if (sched_kbd_raised
+            #if MICROPY_PY_THREAD
+            && mp_thread_is_main_thread()
+            #endif
+            ) {
+            sched_kbd_raised = false;
+            mp_sched_keyboard_interrupt();
+        }
+        #endif
     } else {
         MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
     }
@@ -245,6 +268,11 @@ void mp_handle_pending(mp_handle_pending_behaviour_t behavior) {
         if (obj != MP_OBJ_NULL) {
             MP_STATE_THREAD(mp_pending_exception) = MP_OBJ_NULL;
             if (raise_exceptions) {
+                #if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION && MICROPY_ENABLE_SCHEDULER
+                if (obj == MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(&MP_STATE_VM(mp_kbd_exception))) {
+                    sched_kbd_raised = true;
+                }
+                #endif
                 MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
                 nlr_raise(obj);
             }
-- 
